# Testing and Test-Driven Development

## 1. Test-Driven Development (TDD)

- For new functionality, behavior changes, and bug fixes, follow the Red-Green-Refactor cycle.
- Before implementing a behavior change, identify the expected behavior and acceptance criteria, then write or update tests that verify them.
- Base tests on the requirements and expected behavior, not on assumptions about how the implementation will work.
- Run the new or updated tests before changing production code. Confirm that they fail for the intended reason, such as missing functionality or a reproducible bug.
- After confirming the expected failure, implement the smallest complete solution that satisfies the requirements and makes the tests pass.
- After the tests pass, refactor when beneficial and rerun the relevant tests.

## 2. Test Integrity

- Treat tests as executable specifications of the required behavior.
- Never modify, remove, weaken, or skip a test merely to make an incorrect implementation pass.
- When a test fails, determine whether the cause is an implementation defect, an incorrect test, changed requirements, or a test-environment problem.
- If a test appears incorrect or conflicts with the requirements, explain the specific issue and obtain my approval before changing its expected behavior.
- Do not silently change assertions, expected values, test inputs, or coverage to conceal regressions.
- Do not use mocks, stubs, or fabricated results that bypass the behavior the test is supposed to verify.
- Use mocks only when appropriate to isolate external dependencies or nondeterministic behavior.

## 3. Test Quality and Coverage

- Test observable behavior and externally meaningful contracts rather than implementation details.
- Cover relevant normal cases, edge cases, invalid inputs, error handling, and boundary conditions.
- For bug fixes, add a regression test that reproduces the original failure and verifies the corrected behavior.
- Prefer deterministic, repeatable tests. Avoid unnecessary reliance on real networks, external services, timing assumptions, or shared mutable state.
- Follow the project's existing test framework, conventions, and infrastructure.
- Do not introduce a new test framework or testing dependency without a justified need.

## 4. Regression Prevention

- Run relevant existing tests after making changes, not only the newly added tests.
- Preserve existing test scenarios, assertions, output formats, and metrics unless the requirements explicitly require a change.
- When changing one component, verify that related components and previously supported scenarios still behave as expected.
- Do not treat passing new tests as proof that existing behavior has been preserved.

## 5. Exceptions

- For documentation-only changes, pure renames, and other changes that do not alter runtime behavior, use appropriate validation instead of creating artificial tests.
- If automated testing is impractical, explain why and use the closest reliable verification method available.
- Never claim that a test passed, failed, or was executed unless you actually ran it.
- Report relevant test results and any scenarios that remain unverified.
